\markup {
  \column {
    \pad-around #1
    \wordwrap \abs-fontsize #10 {
      The fugues collected here belong to the group of keyboard fugues and fughettas catalogued as
      BWV 944–962. Most of them are early works, from Bach's years in Arnstadt and Weimar, and many
      survive only in copies made by others rather than in his own hand. Several were written on themes
      borrowed from other composers, a common way for a young musician to learn the craft by studying
      and reworking the music of established masters: BWV 946, 950 and 951 take their subjects from
      Tommaso Albinoni's trio sonatas Op. 1 (1694), and BWV 954 from the Allegro of the sixth sonata of
      Johann Adam Reinken's \italic { Hortus musicus } (1687). BWV 955 was long regarded as Bach's
      reworking of a fugue by Johann Christoph Erselius, although that attribution has since been
      questioned, while the authenticity of BWV 956 remains doubtful. Taken together, these pieces show
      the young Bach absorbing both the Italian and the North German traditions on his way to the
      mature counterpoint of the \italic { Well-Tempered Clavier } and \italic { The Art of Fugue. }
    }
  }
}
